Category: Knowledge Model
Purpose:
  Defines the evidence-weighted knowledge trust system. Every piece of
  knowledge carries evidence, trust, confidence, and provenance metadata
  so AI decisions are auditable and traceable. Introduces the 4-tier
  lifecycle (OBSERVED to ESTABLISHED), the trust/confidence/evidence
  metadata schema, the evidence type taxonomy, the priority hierarchy
  for conflict resolution, decision override rules, and the
  decision_score model that weights trust against project fit.

Owns: ./

Knowledge Lifecycle:
  Every knowledge entry progresses through four tiers:

  OBSERVED
    AI has seen this pattern in a tmp workspace or project scan.
    Reference form: @insight.* or @tmp.*
    Default metadata: trust.low | status.candidate | confidence.low

  CANDIDATE
    Pattern repeated and appears reusable across contexts.
    Reference form: @candidate.*
    Default metadata: trust.low | status.candidate | confidence.low

  VALIDATED
    Confirmed by passing tests, completed tasks, or human review.
    Reference form: @skill.* / @pattern.* / @architecture.*
    Default metadata: trust.medium | status.validated | confidence.medium

  ESTABLISHED
    Successfully applied across multiple projects with no regressions.
    Reference form: @skill.* / @pattern.* / @architecture.*
    Default metadata: trust.high | status.established | confidence.high

  Promotion Rules:
    [K-L1] Promotion is NEVER automatic — it requires evidence.
    [K-L2] CANDIDATE to VALIDATED requires human approval.
    [K-L3] ESTABLISHED requires multi-project success evidence.
    [K-L4] Downgrades are permitted when evidence is invalidated;
           original evidence is preserved.
    [K-L5] Obsolete references are deprecated, NEVER deleted.

Trust Metadata Schema:
  Every reference MUST carry:

  trust:
    level: low | medium | high | established
    confidence: low | medium | high
    human_verified: true | false
    production_verified: true | false

  evidence_count:
    total: <integer>
    by_type:
      code: <integer>
      tests: <integer>
      tasks: <integer>
      decisions: <integer>
      projects: <integer>
      production: <integer>
      external: <integer>
      human: <integer>

  provenance:
    discovered_from: <@reference or @tmp>
    source: <external source / repository / project>
    extracted_by: AI | human | @human
    validated_by: <@reference or @human>
    validated_at: <YYYY-MM-DD>

Evidence Types:
  CODE        — observed in source files
  TEST        — covered by passing tests
  TASK        — applied in a completed task
  DECISION    — referenced by a human-approved decision
  PROJECT     — used in a project context
  PRODUCTION  — deployed and running in production
  EXTERNAL    — cited from an external source (paper, repo, doc)
  HUMAN       — explicitly reviewed or approved by a human

Example:
  @skill.idempotent-command
    purpose: Prevent duplicate command execution.
    evidence:
      - @task.1023
      - @task.1044
      - @project.payment
      - @test.payment-idempotency
    observed_in: tmp/payment-service
    reason: Same external request may be retried.
    status: validated
    trust:
      level: medium
      confidence: high
      human_verified: false
      production_verified: false
    evidence_count:
      total: 4
      by_type:
        task: 2
        project: 1
        test: 1
    provenance:
      discovered_from: @tmp.payment-service
      source: payment-service repository
      extracted_by: AI
      validated_by: @task.1044
      validated_at: 2026-08-31

AI Generated to Truth:
  [TR1] A reference with no evidence MUST have trust.low and
        status.candidate.
  [TR2] AI MUST NOT present low-trust references as authoritative.
  [TR3] Trust level describes evidence breadth, not popularity.
  [TR4] Trust is NOT the automatic winner — applicability and
        project fit also determine selection (see decision_score).

decision_score Model:
  decision_score = trust * applicability * project_fit
  A high-trust pattern that does not fit the project context MAY be
  passed over for a lower-trust, higher-fit alternative. This prevents
  the AI from blindly applying popular patterns everywhere.

Priority Hierarchy:
  When references conflict, resolution follows (highest first):
    1. Explicit project constraint
    2. Approved project decision
    3. Validated project skill
    4. Established organization knowledge
    5. General best practice
    6. AI suggestion

  [PH1] Generic best practice MUST NOT override a project-specific
        decision.
  [PH2] Explicit project constraints always win over global defaults.

Decision Override:
  A project or decision MAY override a reference via:
    overrides:
      - @reference.id
    reason:
      <why the override is required>

  [OV1] Every override MUST include a reason.
  [OV2] Overrides are scoped to the project/decision context only.
  [OV3] AI MUST NOT auto-generate overrides without human review.
  [OV4] Override targets MUST be preserved in the knowledge graph
        as a conflicts-with edge.

Conflict Resolution:
  [CR1] When two references with the same intent conflict, AI MUST
        raise a CONFLICT flag — never guess.
  [CR2] Project-specific decisions resolve conflicts in their domain.
  [CR3] Unresolved conflicts MUST be escalated to human review.
  [CR4] Conflicting references MUST be linked with a conflicts-with
        edge in the knowledge graph.

Unknown Status:
  [UN1] When evidence is insufficient, status MUST be recorded as
        unknown.
  [UN2] AI MUST state "Evidence insufficient to classify" rather than
        guessing.

